WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.563
This tour introduces the VideoForge Studio.

2
00:00:03.563 --> 00:00:13.609
In about three minutes, it covers the tool dock, the workspaces, the timeline, the inspector, the command palette and the keyboard shortcuts.

3
00:00:13.609 --> 00:00:18.810
You will know where everything lives before you start your first project.

4
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:22.435
The tool dock runs down the left edge.

5
00:00:22.435 --> 00:00:24.250
Each icon opens a panel.

6
00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:33.330
Media holds your footage, Effects holds motion presets and transitions, and Graphics holds titles, shapes and templates.

7
00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:38.550
Hover over any icon to see its name, and select it to open the panel.

8
00:00:39.310 --> 00:00:42.934
The workspace switcher sits at the top of the screen.

9
00:00:42.934 --> 00:00:46.764
Each workspace arranges the panels for one kind of work.

10
00:00:46.764 --> 00:00:48.132
Edit is the default.

11
00:00:48.132 --> 00:00:52.646
Animate hides the library and enlarges the timeline for keyframes.

12
00:00:52.646 --> 00:00:57.844
Graphics opens the graphics panel, and Audio gives the timeline more height.

13
00:00:57.844 --> 00:01:00.100
The Studio remembers your choice.

14
00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:05.229
The timeline holds three tracks: graphics, video and audio.

15
00:01:05.229 --> 00:01:08.496
Titles and shapes sit on the graphics track.

16
00:01:08.496 --> 00:01:14.509
Select a clip and press S to split it at the playhead, or drag its edges to trim.

17
00:01:14.509 --> 00:01:23.120
Use the arrow beside a track name to collapse it, and the overview strip above the ruler to move around a long edit.

18
00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:28.353
The inspector on the right shows the settings for whatever is selected.

19
00:01:28.353 --> 00:01:33.205
Use the search box at the top to find a setting by name, for example opacity.

20
00:01:33.205 --> 00:01:35.347
Advanced mode shows every section.

21
00:01:35.347 --> 00:01:41.270
Simple mode hides the rarely used ones, and you can switch between the two from the More menu.

22
00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:46.772
To run any action without hunting through menus, press Control and K.

23
00:01:46.772 --> 00:01:51.249
Type part of a command, such as split or export, and press Enter.

24
00:01:51.249 --> 00:01:59.170
The palette lists the keyboard shortcut next to each command, and it remembers the commands you used most recently.

25
00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:05.930
Press the question mark key at any time to open the full list of keyboard shortcuts.

26
00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:12.442
They are grouped by task: playback, editing, selection, the timeline and the Studio itself.

27
00:02:12.442 --> 00:02:16.950
Learning five or six of them makes editing considerably faster.

28
00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:19.355
That completes the tour.

29
00:02:19.355 --> 00:02:25.630
You now know the dock, the workspaces, the timeline, the inspector and the command palette.

30
00:02:25.630 --> 00:02:32.250
Continue with the Motion graphics tutorial, which builds a complete animated intro from nothing.

